public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Sets the standard discount on new opportunity line items that don't
     * already have one. Lines that already carry a Discount (including 0)
     * are left exactly as entered.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: a single SOQL query no matter how many lines arrive,
     * so 200-line CPQ quotes stay well inside governor limits.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscount(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Only lines missing a discount need their product looked up.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query for every referenced product. PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)
        // runs a SOQL query per call — invoking it per line would issue up to 200
        // queries per quote and exceed the 100-query synchronous limit.
        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>(
            [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]
        );

        // The discount rules stay in PricingRules: we only ask it for the
        // family-level standard discount, we never duplicate its logic.
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                Product2 product = productsById.get(line.Product2Id);
                // If the product can't be found (shouldn't happen — an OLI always
                // references a product), leave the line alone rather than fail the insert.
                if (product != null) {
                    line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(product.Family);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## What I'd change, and why

**The one real change: I don't call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` once per line.** That method executes a SOQL query on every invocation, so a 200-line CPQ quote would issue up to 200 SOQL queries inside one synchronous transaction — past the 100-query governor limit, the insert would fail with `Too many SOQL queries: 101`. It's also a classic N+1 pattern (the same product gets re-queried for every line referencing it). Memoizing per *distinct* product wouldn't fix it either: 200 lines can reference 200 distinct products, so the worst case is still 200 queries.

Instead, the handler collects the distinct `Product2Id`s from lines whose `Discount` is null, runs **one** `SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds`, and then gets each line's discount from `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` — the pricing team's other public method. The rules still live only in `PricingRules` (nothing copied, nothing changed in that class); I just call its family-level entry point instead of its per-product one. Total cost is 1 SOQL query plus an O(1) map lookup per line, so 200-line quotes are comfortably safe.

Other deliberate choices:

- **Only `Discount == null` lines are touched.** A rep-entered `0` is a real value (`0` is not null in Apex), so those lines keep it, per the requirement.
- **`Product2Id != null` guard**, so a product-less line can't make the pricing lookup throw.
- **Graceful degradation:** if a product record somehow isn't returned, the line is left as-is instead of blowing up the whole quote insert.
- **Security:** `with sharing` on the handler (consistent with `PricingRules`), bind variables in SOQL (no injection), no DML — the handler only reads Product2 records already referenced by lines the user is inserting and writes field values on `Trigger.new`, which the platform re-validates on insert. The trigger itself is a thin delegate; all logic lives in the handler.
- **Scope is `before insert` only**, as specified. If the CPQ tool also rewrites existing lines and nulls out `Discount`, we'd add a `before update` path reusing this same method.
- Worth adding a test class covering: null → standard discount applied; `0` and non-zero → untouched; a 200-line bulk insert staying under governor limits; blank product family → `0`.

