public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends renewal reminder emails to opportunity owners.
     *
     * WARNING: This implementation calls Messaging.sendEmail() inside the loop as requested,
     * but note that synchronous Apex (including Schedulable) has a hard governor limit of
     * 10 emails per transaction. Processing 200 opportunities in a single transaction will
     * throw a LimitException after the 10th email. See the notes below the class for the
     * recommended bulk-safe pattern.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds List of Opportunity Ids to process (up to 200)
     * @return Number of emails successfully sent
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Bulk SOQL - retrieve all opportunities in a single query to avoid SOQL-in-loop
        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId, CloseDate
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        Integer successCount = 0;

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();

            // Email the owner (internal user) - does not consume external daily email allocation
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);

            // Plain-text body mentioning the close date
            email.setPlainTextBody(
                'The renewal for this opportunity is due.\n\n' +
                'Opportunity: ' + opp.Name + '\n' +
                'Close Date: ' + opp.CloseDate + '\n\n' +
                'Please review and take appropriate action.'
            );

            try {
                // One call per opportunity as requested.
                // NOTE: This will hit the 10-emails-per-transaction governor limit
                // if more than 10 opportunities are processed in this synchronous context.
                List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results = Messaging.sendEmail(
                    new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> { email }
                );

                if (results != null && !results.isEmpty() && results[0].isSuccess()) {
                    successCount++;
                } else if (results != null && !results.isEmpty()) {
                    // Log specific email errors (e.g., invalid email address, inactive user)
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN, 'Email failed for Opportunity ' + opp.Id +
                        ': ' + results[0].getErrors());
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Catch to prevent one failure from aborting the entire loop.
                // In production, log to a custom object or use Platform Events for observability.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR, 'Exception sending email for Opportunity ' +
                    opp.Id + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return successCount;
    }
}
```

**Why your approach is a bad idea**

1. **Hard Governor Limit (Critical):** You requested one `Messaging.sendEmail` call per opportunity inside the loop. In synchronous Apex (including `Schedulable` classes), Salesforce enforces a limit of **10 emails per transaction**. Even though emails sent to internal users via `setTargetObjectId` do not count against the org’s daily *external* email allocation, they are still subject to the per-transaction limit. With 200 opportunities, the 11th call will throw a `LimitException`, aborting the job (or, because you catch it, silently skipping the remaining ~190 emails). The method will return `10` and the caller will believe the job succeeded.

2. **Inefficient Bulk Pattern:** Calling `sendEmail` individually is slower than batching because each call incurs governor-limit accounting and internal overhead. The bulk API (`Messaging.sendEmail(List<SingleEmailMessage>)`) allows you to send up to **10 emails per call**, and you can make up to 10 calls per transaction (100 emails max synchronously). For 200 emails, you must either:
   - Chunk the opportunities into groups of 10 and use a **Queueable** chain (each Queueable execution gets its own set of limits), or
   - Use a **Batchable** class with a batch size of 10 (or 50), processing each batch in its own execution context.

3. **Misleading Success Count:** If you catch the `LimitException` inside the loop (to satisfy “if one email fails the rest still go out”), the loop will continue but every subsequent `sendEmail` call will also fail. You will return a count of 10 while 190 owners never receive their reminder. You need to detect when you’ve hit the limit and throw a meaningful exception or switch to an asynchronous pattern.

4. **Exception vs. Result Handling:** `Messaging.sendEmail` rarely throws exceptions for business-logic failures (e.g., invalid email address, inactive user). Instead, it returns `SendEmailResult` objects with `isSuccess() == false`. Your code must check the result object (as shown above), not rely solely on `try/catch`.

5. **Hardcoded Strings:** The subject and body use hardcoded English strings. In a production org you should use **Custom Labels** (or Custom Metadata) so the text can be translated and maintained without code changes.

**Recommended Fix (Summary):**
Instead of the loop-with-individual-calls pattern, build a `List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>` for up to 10 opportunities, call `Messaging.sendEmail(emails)` once, inspect the `List<SendEmailResult>`, and then enqueue a **Queueable** (or start a **Batchable**) to process the next chunk of 10. This respects the 10-emails-per-transaction limit while remaining bulk-safe.

I changed one thing about your approach, and it matters: I did **not** load all ~2.3M contacts with `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]`.

Why:
- **Heap limit.** Apex heap is 6 MB synchronous / 12 MB async. 2.3M `Contact` sObjects (even with only 3 fields) is hundreds of MB of heap — every 200-account MDM batch would throw `System.LimitException: Apex heap size too large` and break the integration.
- **Cost/selectivity.** A full `Contact` scan per batch is O(all contacts) even when one account changed country. Filtering by `AccountId IN :changedAccountIds` uses the indexed `AccountId` field and touches only the relevant slice.
- **Your actual requirement still holds.** “No queries in loops” means *don’t SOQL inside the per-record loop*. I still use **exactly one SOQL** (outside the loop) and **one bulk DML**, so it is bulk-safe.
- **Correctness nuance.** Because each account has its *own* previous `BillingCountry`, the `MailingCountry IN :previousCountries` filter is a superset across accounts; I re-apply the exact per-account rule (`blank OR == that account’s previous country`) in the loop, so foreign contacts are still left alone.

I also declared the handler `without sharing`: this is an internal system-level sync driven by an MDM integration, and it must not be silently scoped by the running user’s sharing. All values are bound (no SOQL injection), and only `MailingCountry` is written.

